WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.039 --> 00:00:09.630
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:12.690
So in this section, we
talk about calorimetry.

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:16.830
In contrast to the discussion
of tracking detectors,

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:19.680
here what we're trying
to do is measure

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:21.240
the energy of the particles.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.640
And we do this by
basically destroying them.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:26.410
The underlying content is
rather straightforward.

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:27.580
We have a particle.

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:30.370
And we put a piece of
material in front of it,

00:00:30.370 --> 00:00:33.180
such that it slams
into it, and the energy

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:36.340
deposited by the particle is
the energy, the measurement

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:40.440
we try to undertake.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.650
So in nuclear and
particle physics,

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:46.130
that is exactly what we
refer to as calorimetrics.

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:48.770
So the detection of particles
for measuring the properties

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:53.630
through the total absorption
in the block of matter.

00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:56.210
The common feature, or
the central feature,

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:58.730
is that the measurement
is destructive.

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:00.920
So again, in tracking
detectors, we

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:04.099
try to minimally
disturb the particle

00:01:04.099 --> 00:01:08.150
and in calorimetrics,
we try to destroy them.

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:11.210
The exception to this
might be a muon which

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:13.970
might at high energies
deposit only a small fraction

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:15.920
of its energy in
the calorimeter,

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:18.380
or a neutrino, which
just flies through

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:20.660
without having any interaction.

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:23.960
But the purpose is really
to measure energies

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:25.670
by destroying the particle.

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:28.300
And it's widely used in all
kinds of areas of particle

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:29.420
and nuclear physics.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:32.630
Neutrino experiments, proton
decay experiments, cosmic ray

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:36.110
detectors, collider
experiments, and so on.

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:38.900
And in collider experiments
specifically, the idea

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:42.290
is to build the detector such
that it completely surrounds

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:46.040
the interaction region, such
that you don't lose energy

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:51.320
from the collision just passing
through an uninstrumented

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:53.050
region.

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:56.980
The detection mechanism
can vary quite a bit.

00:01:56.980 --> 00:01:58.490
We use scintillators a lot.

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:02.530
We use silicon in
some modern detectors.

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:05.650
With ionization, we use
Cherenkov detection.

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:10.000
We use sometimes
cryogenic detectors,

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:13.000
which are very sensitive to
very small energy depositions,

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:14.890
and they can be quite useful.

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:17.770
They are used in dark
matter experiments

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:20.710
or on neutrino [INAUDIBLE]
decay experiments like so.

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:26.880
Again, conceptually,
you can differentiate

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:30.450
between homogeneous
calorimetrics and sampling

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:31.710
calorimeters.

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:35.770
The homogeneous calorimeters,
basically the entire absorbable

00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:39.570
material is equal or is the
same as the detector material.

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:44.640
So an example for this is lead
glass, which is often used.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:46.590
So what you do then
in the calorimeter

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:50.490
is you induce electromagnetic
and nuclear showers

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:52.750
and then the energy
of the incoming party

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:55.890
is converted into photons.

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:57.690
And then what you need
is a photodetector,

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:01.740
which then measures the
number of photons coming out

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:04.770
of your detector material.

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:09.060
For this to work, the detector
needs to be transparent.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:11.950
Alternatively, one can
use sampling experiment,

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:16.380
sampling detectors, where you
have the heavy material being

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:19.960
used in order to induce a shower
and then the detection material

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:21.960
in order to count, again,
the number of photons.

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:28.590
So homogeneous calorimeter
have typically very good energy

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:29.297
resolution.

00:03:29.297 --> 00:03:31.380
And the reason for this
is that nothing gets lost.

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:33.570
Everything is being measured
in the absorber, which

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:35.430
is the detector.

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:38.040
But that leads then
to some limitations.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:40.710
For example, that the
granularity of the detector

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:42.330
is typically limited.

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:44.340
And then there's no
longitudinal information

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:45.630
about the shower development.

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:47.370
You basically have one block.

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:50.490
For example, a left
blockage is shown here,

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:53.280
the tungsten block,
which is shown here,

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:56.360
are used for the measurement.

00:03:56.360 --> 00:03:59.277
So you produce photons,
and so then the photons

00:03:59.277 --> 00:04:00.110
need to be measured.

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:03.080
And then that's done
with photodetectors.

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:06.140
Here, the requirements,
the range of requirements,

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:07.100
is quite big.

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:10.850
Sometimes you want to be able
to measure every single photon

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:13.820
so the quantum efficiency
needs to be quite high.

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:15.980
In other detectors,
you need to be

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:19.320
able to put this detector in
a radiation hot environment.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:21.200
And so that then changes.

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:25.370
The main types available are the
old-fashioned photomultiplier

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:29.055
tubes, which actually become
quite sophisticated, PMTs.

00:04:29.055 --> 00:04:31.340
There's gas-based
photodetectors.

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:34.890
There's solid-state detectors,
which are quite popular,

00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:38.120
so-called SIPM
silicon photodetectors

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:42.750
or some hybrid modules of those.

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:47.310
So the energy resolution
in a calorimeter

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:48.730
depends on a number of things.

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:51.750
As I was saying, one measures
the number of particles

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:53.490
being produced in a shower.

00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:56.220
And so that's just a
counting experiment.

00:04:56.220 --> 00:05:00.095
And the uncertainty
of that scales

00:05:00.095 --> 00:05:01.970
to the square root of
the number of particles

00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:03.330
produced or measured.

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:06.860
And so here, we have
a square root n term.

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:08.840
So the relative
energy measurement

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:14.010
has an arrow, which is with 1
over square root of the energy.

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:16.020
And then there's
more contributions.

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:19.560
There's constant terms, which
come from inhomogeneities.

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:21.240
Those are elements
where there's just

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:26.790
no detector, no equipment in
the direction of the party.

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:29.580
But those can be overlapped
regions or regions

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:32.430
where you have two detector
modules being glued together.

00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:33.490
You don't measure there.

00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:36.600
And that leads to them, a
constant term in the energy

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:37.890
resolution.

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:39.900
And then when you
translate the signal,

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:46.110
the electromagnetic signals
into an electronic signal,

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:48.480
there can be noise induced,
and that noise then

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:51.900
leads to a typical turn, which
goes with one over the energy.

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:55.500
So very classical, you
have those three components

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:56.490
to the energy--

00:05:56.490 --> 00:05:59.280
three components to
the energy resolution.

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:01.410
One is this 1 over the
square root of the energy.

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:06.280
One is constant, and one is
depending on 1 over the energy.

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:08.190
And so when you
design a detector,

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:11.610
you want to place it
such or design it such

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:13.470
that the most
important physics you

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:15.960
want to do with this
detector is optimize

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:18.680
towards those components.

